Here is the commit message:
sctp: change to check peer prsctp_capable when using prsctp
 polices

Now before using prsctp polices, sctp uses asoc->prsctp_enable to
check if prsctp is enabled. However asoc->prsctp_enable is set only
means local host support prsctp, sctp should not abandon packet if
peer host doesn't enable prsctp.

So this patch is to use asoc->peer.prsctp_capable to check if prsctp
is enabled on both side, instead of asoc->prsctp_enable, as asoc's
peer.prsctp_capable is set only when local and peer both enable prsctp.

Fixes: a6c2f792873a ("sctp: implement prsctp TTL policy")
Signed-off-by: Xin Long <lucien.xin@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: David S. Miller <davem@davemloft.net>

Here is the root cause:
The bug was caused by the incorrect assumption that the local host's support for PR-SCTP (indicated by `asoc->prsctp_enable`) was sufficient to use PR-SCTP policies. This led to situations where packets could be processed under PR-SCTP policies even if the peer host did not support PR-SCTP, potentially causing packet abandonment or incorrect handling of messages. The check should have verified that both the local and peer hosts are capable of PR-SCTP, which is indicated by `asoc->peer.prsctp_capable`.

